- How would you optimize for large n or multiple queries on the same array?
- How would you handle large counts and modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design DP states that encode ordering constraints across variable-length tokens, a common real-world pattern in data parsing and tokenization.

## Key Concepts
- DP state design with (pos, prevLen) to compare adjacent tokens
- String comparison for equal-length numbers; no leading zeros handling
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs for clarity and performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const memo = new Map();
  const dfs = (pos, prevLen) => {
    if (pos === n) return 1;
    const key = pos + ':' + prevLen;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    let res = 0;
    for (let nextLen = 1; nextLen <= n - pos; nextLen++) {
      if (s[pos] === '0') break;
      if (prevLen === 0 || nextLen > prevLen || (nextLen === prevLen && s.substring(pos, pos + nextLen) > s.substring(pos - prevLen, pos))) {
        res = (res + dfs(pos + nextLen, nextLen)) % mod;
      }
    }
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  };
  return dfs(0, 0);
}
```

